{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/planning/review-protocol.md docs/planning/dependencies.md docs/architecture/review-verdict.schema.json'","aggregated_output":"# Independent review and evidence protocol\n\nApplies to G00/G01 as planning/design reviews, every implementation chunk and\nevery phase. Scope the verdict honestly: a plan review evaluates the plan and\nfuture gates, not application code or tests that do not exist.\n\nG00/G01 were reviewed by Claude Code; those records are historical and cover\nonly their recorded inputs. Under D026, Claude Code implements and Codex is the\nindependent reviewer for C01–C26 and the phase reviews P01–P08.\n\n## Gate\n\nActual independent Codex review must score at least 9/10 overall and in each\ncritical dimension: architecture, behavior, authority_security, validation.\nNo blocking or required fixes may remain. Missing mandatory evidence, stale\ninputs, or a mismatched receipt prevents completion independently of scores.\n\nThe implementer cannot substitute its own score or edit the reviewer's verdict.\nDo not ask a reviewer to raise a score; fix findings and request a new review.\nRetain all attempts and explain remediation. Optional suggestions may remain\nonly when explicitly classified as nonrequired and consistent with the plan.\n\n## Dimensions\n\n- **Architecture:** depth at domain interfaces; locality; justified seams/real\n  adapters; authoritative ownership; no needless pass-through layers or hidden\n  cross-module coupling. Use the glossary and accepted ADRs.\n- **Behavior:** complete chunk requirements, exact working-v1 subset, correct\n  success/denied/empty/stale/partial/unknown outcomes, and no invented support.\n- **Authority/security:** context/credential isolation, review/approval binding,\n  trusted hosted assessment, protected storage, safe external destinations,\n  bounded execution, provenance, Node-free shipped backend, and secret hygiene.\n- **Validation:** meaningful production-interface tests; independent external\n  expectations; required native/live/artifact evidence; reproducibility, hashes,\n  explicit limitations, and causal failure cases. Test counts alone earn no score.\n\nScores below 9 require concrete findings with locations, impact, and acceptance\nconditions. Even a 9/10 verdict with a required fix cannot pass.\n\nOwner rule D034 (2026-10-18): a chunk may complete on a review scoring at least\n8 in every dimension with no blockers; its remaining findings are recorded as\naccepted failures. The reviewer still scores against the 9/10 gate above. At\nthe end of each phase every chunk accepted below 9 is remediated and\nre-reviewed until it passes the full gate, before the phase review.\n\n## Phase reviews\n\nAfter the last chunk of a phase completes, request a cumulative Codex review of\nthat phase (P01: C01–C03, P02: C04–C07, P03: C08–C12, P04: C13–C15, P05: C16–C18,\nP06: C19–C22, P07: C23–C24, P08: C25–C26). The phase packet binds the current\ncommitted tree, every chunk receipt in the phase and earlier phases' receipts,\nthe phase's integrated test/native results rerun against that tree, and the\ndiff since the previous phase. The reviewer assesses integration across the\nphase and the earlier features it affects. The same five-score threshold and\nempty required_fixes/blockers/evidence_gaps apply, with no owner acceptance\n(D034), and every member chunk must hold a passing 9/10 completion. The next phase does not\nbegin until its predecessor phase review passes. Historical chunk scores are\ninputs to assess, never a substitute.\n\n## Review transport\n\nThe controller writes an immutable request packet (request/attempt IDs, chunk or\nphase, scope, predecessor receipts, source/dependency/build-flag/evidence\nfingerprints, exact commands/results/evidence paths, diff, limitations, the\nverdict schema and one absolute verdict path), then delivers it:\n\n- Preferred: the owner-designated Codex thread, addressed by exact ID through\n  `codex queue --thread <id> --message <text>`; never `--last`. A prior\n  reviewer-authored handshake file proves routing. Queue success is submission\n  only. The controller waits with bounded progress for the matching verdict.\n- Fallback: a fresh `codex exec --sandbox read-only --ignore-user-config\n  --ignore-rules --ephemeral` reviewer with web search disabled, approval\n  policy never, `--output-schema` and captured JSONL stdout/stderr. It is\n  recorded as a fresh reviewer under a new attempt; the prior attempt is\n  expired so a late verdict cannot advance the gate. No bypass flags.\n\nThe verdict must validate against the strict schema, echo the request ID and\nsupplied fingerprints, and the frozen inputs must be unchanged afterwards.\n\n## Receipt fields\n\nRecord review ID, chunk/gate/phase ID, attempt, request ID, transport (thread\nID or fresh exec), time, actual harness/version/model when reported, input prompt hash, source fingerprint, dependency manifest hash,\ncheck/evidence manifest hash, raw output hash, reviewer scores, findings,\nrequired fixes, blocking findings, limitations, and whether source stayed frozen.\nFor final packages also bind pre-sign/post-sign/stapled artifact hashes and the\ninstalled-byte inventory. A review of a different tree or package cannot pass.\n\nThe input fingerprint includes tracked/nonignored source, instruction files,\nconfiguration, file modes/symlink targets, and exact compiled dependency inputs.\nDocument narrow exclusions for review outputs/mutable status to avoid circular\nhashes. Exclusions cannot hide application sources, lockfiles, generated bindings,\nbuild flags, fixture rules, tests, notices, or package inventories. Evidence and\nstatus hashes remain independently recorded even when excluded from source hash.\n\nUse actual Codex review. The fresh fallback runs read-only, ephemeral, without\nuser configuration/rules, web search, hooks or connectors; the controller verifies\nthe effective settings it can observe rather than assuming the sandbox disables\nevery tool. The preferred thread's own sandbox is not controlled by this project;\nits read-only obligation is instructed and enforced by unchanged pre/post\nfingerprints. The reviewer may write only its designated review artifacts.\nThe reviewer reads project guidance and the exact scoped files/evidence deliberately. Evidence hashes detect\nstale/tampered records; they are not cryptographic proof of provider authorship.\n\n## Completion and invalidation\n\nAll predecessors must have passing receipts. Checks must be executed against\nthe same inputs and exact intended feature/build flags. Record each result as\npassed, failed, blocked, or not applicable with a reason. An unavailable required\ncheck is blocked, never silently not applicable.\n\nOn a failed gate: keep chunk active, fix findings, rerun affected checks plus\nany regression surface the fix reaches, freeze new inputs, and obtain a fresh\nindependent review. A dependency update reopens affected consumers. Final release\nrevalidates cumulative behavior and source-bound prior evidence; it cannot merely\nsum historical 9s from incompatible versions.\n\nCompletion is followed by a focused commit whose tree is verified against the\nreviewed source. Changes to source after review require a new relevant review.\nGit publication uses normal pushes to the specified v2 remote; no force-push.\n\n## Evidence handling\n\nRepository summaries contain reproducible commands, environments, hashes,\nnonsecret fixture IDs, pass/fail/blocked status, and limitations. Keep real\ncredentials, prompts/responses, tokens, Keychain exports, unredacted traces,\ncustomer records, signing secrets, and signed-in browser captures private.\nRedact output before publication; retain original sensitive evidence in private\noperator storage with a hash reference when appropriate.\n\nThe C01 controller must reject a score8 in any dimension, required fixes despite\nscore9, missing check results, changed dependency/source hashes, edited raw output,\nduplicate/invalid receipt IDs, changed build flags, malformed schemas, and missing\nnative/artifact proof. Completion state is not writable by a reviewer or inferred\nfrom a successful CLI exit code or queue acknowledgement alone. It also rejects\na verdict whose request ID or echoed fingerprints differ from the packet, a\nverdict for an expired attempt, and a phase completion lacking a passing phase\nreview.\n# Dependency and source gates\n\nArchitecture proposed exact first-slice versions in docs/architecture/qualification.md.\nAs of C01 (2026-10-17) the desktop graph is resolved, locked and compiled on\nthe macOS ARM64 host; the resolved inputs are recorded below. Later SDK\nfamilies and other inputs remain gated at their consuming chunks.\n\n## Source ownership\n\n| Input          | Inspected revision                       | Ownership/use                                           |\n|----------------|------------------------------------------|---------------------------------------------------------|\n| cdot-framework | e3553b27a3d6bf457a2e585c5ae479c7db89a17b | Selective patterns/modules after provenance evaluation  |\n| Desktop v1     | 74754c02b19d87187b6dca81f64550a669b0f841 | Behavioral/contracts reference; preserve checkout       |\n| Academy        | 7f3a28c4082a5bb278bbff5d79fab106c240155a | Shared React source; hosted learning authority          |\n| Native Harness | 9331a5839bc6a763d94dee7584746f58bbbe1181 | Desktop-capable native engine/protocol input            |\n| SDK Phase 3    | ec6230d2a19b9c835c9e4c9487795b38ad538af3 | Accepted scan subset; native/live qualification pending |\n| scm-sdk crate  | crates.io 0.1.0, sha256 6d555d955c3734dd5ee78f7173569bdecd4426095b2ffedc4b77d8f0f2c2e8d9, from 4f65dc48686a3b701ed0585812fcca46859f7e5a | D027 scan input from C02 (scan feature only); 164 package unit tests pass natively; live qualification is C25 |\n| SDK Phase 4    | 32cbcb6d9b04478aeca21e767ed6ddb8949ec7bb | Accepted management/DLP/IAM subset; qualify exact uses  |\n\nDo not consume an active neighboring worktree via an unpinned path and later\nclaim the accepted commit was built. Obtain a clean revision-specific source\nsnapshot/package without secrets or ignored local configuration. Manifest both\nits origin revision and actual source/package hash. A dependency update reopens\nthe affected contract/native/artifact checks and review. Never fabricate SDK\nmethod parity from a similar TypeScript method name.\n\n## C01 resolved inputs\n\nRecorded by C01 and bound to its review. Locks: `Cargo.lock`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`,\n`rust-toolchain.toml`, `docs/architecture/toolchain.json`,\n`docs/architecture/inputs.lock.json`; the controller's dependency fingerprint\nhashes them with the consumed `.inputs/` bytes and tool identities.\n\n| Input | Resolved | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Rust toolchain | 1.99.0 (b940084d7 2026-09-28) | Corrected from 1.90.0; proc-macro dylib dyld failure at deployment target 13.0 on macOS 27 (evidence/C01/toolchain-correction.json). Release `strip` removed for the same failure. |\n| Tauri / tauri-build / Tauri CLI / @tauri-apps/api | 2.12.4 / 2.7.3 / 2.12.3 / 2.12.4 | `custom-protocol` added by the Tauri CLI for bundles |\n| ts-rs | 12.0.1 (serde-compat, no-serde-warnings) | Generated declarations committed; `xtask bindings --check` |\n| React / ReactDOM | 19.2.3 | Single instance per consumer (pnpm strict peers) |\n| Tailwind / Vite / Vitest / plugin-react / TypeScript | 4.3.2 / 7.3.6 / 3.2.6 / 5.2.0 / 5.9.3 | Matched to Academy's resolved lock instead of the earlier 4.1.18 / 7.3.0 proposal |\n| Build Node / pnpm | 24.21.0 / 10.6.5 (corepack shim in `.tools/bin`) | Build-only; never shipped |\n| Academy shared presentation | `ai-security-academy` 891f65eef76b53eb87155d0f0f5622228e217a2d on branch `v2/desktop-presentation` (base 7f3a28c4) | Pushed to the Academy origin on 2026-10-17 (owner-approved; main untouched). `xtask fetch-inputs --source <academy clone>` materializes `.inputs/academy` (tree sha256 1c4ce702…); a fresh clone from origin reproduces it (evidence/C01/clean-checkout). |\n\nThird-party notices for the shipped graph are in `docs/architecture/notice-inventory.json`\n(`xtask notices --check`). The Academy package is Academy-owned and recorded by\nits pinned commit, not relicensed.\n\n## Required gates\n\n- **Distribution terms:** G01 records owner-approved v2 source/binary terms,\n  Academy redistribution permission and every framework-derived import's\n  disposition under [distribution.md](distribution.md). No extraction before\n  clearance; C26 verifies notices/terms against exact shipped inventory.\n\n- **Provenance:** evaluate each direct import's actual license/notice and allowed\n  usage. cdot-framework is not blanket MIT and its billing-retention condition\n  cannot be erased by renaming. Prefer independently implemented patterns where\n  direct copying is unsuitable. Academy sharing is owner-directed; establish\n  package ownership and third-party notices rather than inventing a license.\n- **Academy packaging:** React19/Tailwind4/Vite7 are observed in Academy, while\n  the framework uses React18/Tailwind3. Select a compatible pinned desktop stack\n  and one shared presentation source. The existing UI package re-exports app\n  source and is not a ready independently reusable full learner app.\n- **Shared-source changes:** propose the smallest extraction that supports actual\n  web/desktop consumers. Keep Academy web behavior and hosted contracts stable;\n  run both projects' affected tests. Pin the reviewed shared revision/package\n  before completing its consuming desktop chunk.\n- **SDK scan:** ec6230d2's accepted subset supplies synchronous scan. It requires\n  explicit scan credentials; management OAuth is separate. Accepted Linux\n  reports do not establish macOS ARM64 support. Qualify the native compiler/TLS\n  dependencies, runtime integration, scanner contracts, cancellation, sanitized\n  errors, and unknown outcomes in C02–C03.\n- **SDK administration:** 32cbcb6d's accepted Phase4 is a candidate input for\n  C08–C12. Map every actual operation, option, pagination bound, and error to\n  accepted source/tests. SDK family labels are insufficient proof of parity.\n- **SDK later families:** Phase5 Red Team/Model and Phase6 Gateway were developing\n  or pending during inspection. Their exact accepted pins are prerequisites to\n  C13/C16–C18. Do not consume unfinished work or substitute mocks as completion.\n  Stable v2 must qualify the SDK subset it actually uses, including native/live\n  evidence; it need not claim every unrelated SDK method is complete.\n- **Harness:** retain the desktop-capable native engine/protocol after source and\n  package checks. Do not substitute an older canonical checkout or npm launcher.\n  Rebuild/reseal v2 resources to omit Node/managed CLI where proven unnecessary;\n  old v1 resource hashes do not attest the changed v2 bundle. Preserve inference\n  and MCP routing/context/tool restrictions.\n- **Native host:** select pinned Tauri2/Rust/toolchain dependencies compatible\n  with macOS ARM64 and the chosen SDK. Resolve one application-owned async\n  runtime strategy; do not create an independent runtime per request.\n- **Identity:** specify independent v2 bundle/display/data/Keychain identities in\n  G01. Test v1/v2 coexistence, reset, uninstall, import, and rollback. Do not\n  silently reuse a v1 namespace or change the learner's remote account.\n- **Live environment:** identify designated test tenants/accounts, scopes,\n  reversible resources, budgets, and cleanup/readback before C25 execution.\n  Prod configuration discovery is not mutation authorization. Missing access is\n  a real dependency gap; never report skipped tests as passed.\n- **Hosted OAuth registrations:** G01 maps reuse/new-client/redirect/scope needs\n  for Academy device authorization, Harness company SSO and MCP. Responsible\n  Academy/Gateway/IdP operators own authorized staging/configuration changes and\n  deployment. V2 identity alone neither proves a new registration is required\n  nor authorizes production changes. Resolve before the consuming live tests.\n- **Signing:** identify an authorized Developer ID, provisioning/entitlement\n  requirements, notarization access, and a native acceptance host. Access secrets\n  only at use time through approved storage, never committed plan variables.\n\n## Official host references\n\nTauri supports Rust commands and async messages between presentation and host;\nthe selected command/module interface still requires G01 design and review.\n[Calling Rust](https://v2.tauri.app/develop/calling-rust/)\n\nWindow/WebView capability grants require deliberate scoping; renderer grants do\nnot replace Rust's application authority checks.\n[Capabilities](https://v2.tauri.app/security/capabilities/)\n\nDMG distribution and macOS signing are separate from successful compilation.\n[DMG packaging](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/dmg/),\n[macOS signing/notarization](https://v2.tauri.app/distribute/sign/macos/).\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema\",\n  \"title\": \"Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 independent review verdict\",\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"additionalProperties\": false,\n  \"required\": [\n    \"request_id\",\n    \"attempt_id\",\n    \"subject\",\n    \"fingerprints\",\n    \"scores\",\n    \"required_fixes\",\n    \"blockers\",\n    \"evidence_gaps\",\n    \"optional_suggestions\",\n    \"limitations\",\n    \"inspected_files\",\n    \"recommendation\"\n  ],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"request_id\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"attempt_id\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\"\n    },\n    \"subject\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"description\": \"Chunk C01-C44 or phase P01-P09 under review\"\n    },\n    \"fingerprints\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"additionalProperties\": false,\n      \"required\": [\n        \"source\",\n        \"dependencies\",\n        \"build_flags\",\n        \"evidence\"\n      ],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"source\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"dependencies\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"build_flags\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        },\n        \"evidence\": {\n          \"type\": \"string\"\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"scores\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"additionalProperties\": false,\n      \"required\": [\n        \"architecture\",\n        \"behavior\",\n        \"authority_security\",\n        \"validation\",\n        \"overall\"\n      ],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"architecture\": {\n          \"type\": \"integer\",\n          \"minimum\": 1,\n          \"maximum\": 10\n        },\n        \"behavior\": {\n          \"type\": \"integer\",\n          \"minimum\": 1,\n          \"maximum\": 10\n        },\n        \"authority_security\": {\n          \"type\": \"integer\",\n          \"minimum\": 1,\n          \"maximum\": 10\n        },\n        \"validation\": {\n          \"type\": \"integer\",\n          \"minimum\": 1,\n          \"maximum\": 10\n        },\n        \"overall\": {\n          \"type\": \"integer\",\n          \"minimum\": 1,\n          \"maximum\": 10\n        }\n      }\n    },\n    \"required_fixes\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"blockers\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"evidence_gaps\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"optional_suggestions\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"limitations\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"inspected_files\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"type\": \"string\"\n      }\n    },\n    \"recommendation\": {\n      \"type\": \"string\",\n      \"enum\": [\n        \"pass\",\n        \"fail\"\n      ]\n    }\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat GLOSSARY.md docs/planning/chunks/C30.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/invariants.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/state-ownership.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/inbox-reads.md docs/architecture/ui-v2/screens/shell.md'","aggregated_output":"# Prisma AIRS Desktop v2 domain vocabulary\n\nThese terms name the desktop product and its native authority concepts.\nMechanisms, wire formats and transition rules belong in the selected design.\n\n## Language\n\n**Tenant profile**: An application-owned configuration for a Prisma AIRS tenant,\nincluding its label, endpoints, and protected credential references. A scan-only\nprofile does not require management OAuth credentials. A profile is not an\nAcademy learner account or a Gateway workspace.\n\n**Credential capability**: The specific operations a credential can authorize,\nsuch as scanning, management, inference, or workspace MCP. Possessing one\ncapability does not establish another; configuration keeps them explicit.\n\n**Gateway workspace**: A remote AI Gateway administrative/inference context within\nthe selected tenant profile. It is distinct from a local filesystem project.\n\n**Configuration review**: An operator-visible proposal for an AIRS configuration\nchange, bound to the current tenant, credential context, target resource, and\ndraft. Rust owns its validity. Displaying or importing a proposal does not grant\npermission to apply it.\n\n**Remote outcome**: What is known about an attempted remote action: not sent,\nrejected, completed as established by evidence, or unknown. Local timeout,\ncancellation, and disconnection do not establish remote rollback.\n\n**Academy feature**: The learner experience within the broader desktop app.\nPresentation is shared with Academy; remote learning contracts remain hosted.\nLearning progression, XP, cosmetic credits, awards, and other gamification stay\nwithin this feature.\n\n**Academy learner account**: The remote learning identity owning progress,\nassessments, certificates, and account-scoped local drafts/downloads. It is\nindependent of the selected Prisma AIRS tenant and Harness inference sign-in.\n\n**Shared presentation**: React controls, themes, and shell layout reused from the\nAcademy-owned source. Sharing appearance does not share learner authority,\ngamification effects, manager routes, or browser-session assumptions globally.\n\n**Harness**: The assistant feature using the native Rust agent engine and\ndesktop-owned lifecycle, credential grants, reviews, and protected history.\nThe SCM SDK is a separate dependency, not the Harness engine.\n\n**Project scope**: The explicitly selected local filesystem scope and approved\ntool permissions available to a Harness conversation. Preserve the existing\nrestrictions initially; a transcript is not permission to restore approvals.\n\n**MCP grant**: A desktop-owned authorization context for a specific remote MCP\nbinding. Inference sign-in is separate. Discovery does not establish execution;\nexecution evidence must correspond to the actual approved call.\n\n**Historical conversation**: Saved user work that can be inspected after import.\nResumption requires separate context/protocol compatibility validation. It never\nrestores pending approvals or old authentication sessions.\n\n**Billing**: Desktop commercial charging/monetization machinery, excluded from\nv2. Remote Gateway usage/cost/budget/limit administration remains product\nfunctionality. Academy's earned learning economy remains Academy-local.\n\n**Shipped backend runtime**: Executables/workers required by the installed app's\nbackend. Node/Bun and TypeScript backend workers are excluded. Build-time JS\ntooling and React JavaScript executing in the WebView are separate concepts.\n\n**Parity**: Coverage of agreed working v1 behavior with new v2 evidence. V1 source\nimplementation, retained fixture/contract results, retained live evidence, and\nv2 installed-artifact results are distinct. Replacing a preview/stub with a live\nfeature is scope expansion, not implicit parity work.\n\n**Authority principal**: The native-established operator or restricted Harness\nidentity on whose behalf desktop work is requested. A claimed identity in a\nrequest is not an Authority principal.\n\n**Authority handle**: A principal-scoped entry to native desktop operations and\nobservations. Possessing an operation reference does not expand its authority.\n\n**Intent**: A typed request for a specific desktop action under the caller's\ncurrent authority. It is distinct from a Harness proposal or an arbitrary remote\nrequest.\n\n**Receipt**: Native acknowledgement that an Intent was admitted, with its request\nor operation reference. It is not evidence of operator confirmation or remote\ncompletion.\n\n**Refusal**: Native rejection of an Intent before admission under the requested\nauthority. A remote action's failure is a Remote outcome instead.\n\n**ViewScope**: The product context a caller asks to observe, such as a learner\naccount, tenant workspace or Harness conversation. Native authority determines\nwhich part is visible to that caller.\n\n**Observation**: A scoped redacted view of native desktop state and operation\nprogress. It confers no execution or credential authority.\n\n**ContextRef**: A native reference to the particular product context presented\nto the caller. Returning to the same tenant or account does not revive retired\nauthority.\n\n**Delivery session**: The current native-registered channel for one caller's\nIntent deliveries. It is separate from tenant, learner or inference identity.\n\n**Delivery sequence**: The order identity of an Intent within its Delivery session,\nused to distinguish duplicate delivery from an explicit new action.\n_Avoid_: Business retry, authorization token\n\n**Native-owned payload reference**: A scoped reference to staged desktop input\nwhose ownership and content are known natively. It is not permission to read an\narbitrary filesystem path.\n\n**Reviewed**: A Configuration review whose captured proposal remains eligible for\noperator confirmation.\n\n**Confirming**: A Configuration review currently awaiting the native operator's\nanswer for that specific proposal.\n\n**Consumed**: A Configuration review already committed to its single admitted\noperation. Failure of that operation does not make the review reusable.\n\n**Queued**: An admitted desktop operation whose remote dispatch has not been\nclaimed.\n\n**DispatchClaimed**: A desktop operation for which remote dispatch may have begun.\nLocal cancellation at this stage does not prove NotSent.\n\n**Settled**: A desktop operation with a recorded terminal local result and its\ncurrent Remote outcome. Later reconciliation may refine knowledge of that outcome.\n\n**Harness grant**: Native authority for one verified inference identity and its\napproved tenant/workspace/origin context. Rotating that identity's token material\nis distinct from replacing or revoking the Harness grant.\n\n**Grant epoch**: The particular lifetime of a native grant's authority. Retired\nauthority stays retired even when an equivalent identity is selected again.\n\n**One-time secret receipt**: The operator-scoped acknowledgement holding newly\nissued secret material for its explicit transient reveal. It is separate from a\nstored credential lookup.\n\n**Editor lease**: The hold on the reviewed document while native operator\nconfirmation is pending. A changed document or context invalidates that hold.\n# C30 — Inbox, reads, tenant switch guard, Home attention\n\nPhase: P09. Status: planned. Required predecessor: C29.\n\n## Outcome\n\nInbox items, reads and acknowledgments are core-owned per tenant; the single tenant-switch path refuses while a Harness decision is pending; Home shows attention and read status from the same scopes.\n\n## Inputs and dependency gates\n\nC29; screens/inbox-reads.md, screens/shell.md. Read the [common execution contract](../IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md),\n[review protocol](../review-protocol.md), and [test catalogue](../test-catalogue.md).\nRead the [UI v2 specification](../../architecture/ui-v2/DESIGN-V2.md) and its\ncompanions; the frozen prototype under `docs/architecture/ui-v2/prototype/` is\nan immutable input. Freeze all source/shared dependency inputs.\n\n## Milestones\n\n1. Scopes `InboxItem`, `ReadStatus`; intents `readTenant`, `recheckProduct`, `acknowledgeItem`, `snoozeItem`, `unsnoozeItem`, `switchTenant{resolution?}` with `SwitchBlocked` refusal.\n2. Inbox screen (tabs, rows, detail pane, contextual action targets), Home attention list and read-status line, completion toasts derived from `ReadStatus` transitions, guard dialog rendered from the refusal.\n3. Snooze return times computed in core; one `snoozeLabel` shared by Home and Inbox.\n4. Fixtures for R2Inbox and the fresh-tenant state.\n\n## Required acceptance\n\n- Invariants I1, I2, I4, I11, I12 with V2-R01, R02, R04, R08, R09 and V2-T01, T02, T04, T12, T13; native V2-N01, N02.\n- Tenant-switch workload: new tenant's Home within 100 ms from cached scopes (registered in budgets.json).\n\n## Evidence and completion\n\nAs C28, plus the workload result.\n\n## Safety and recovery\n\nFixtures and mock runtime only; no live calls.\n# Normative invariants\n\nThese twelve rules are the behaviours Codex forced into the prototype over\nsixteen review rounds. They are normative: an implementation chunk that\nviolates one fails its behaviour or authority dimension regardless of how the\nscreen looks. Code shape is free; the rule and its test are not.\n\nEach rule names its acceptance test id. `V2-Txx` tests are Vitest component\ntests in the desktop app (fixture-driven); `V2-Rxx` are Rust integration tests\nin desktop-core; `V2-Nxx` are native acceptance checks added to\n`native-checks.json` by the chunk that implements the rule.\n\n| Id | Rule | Tests | Chunk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| I1 | There is exactly one tenant-switch path. The core refuses a switch while a Harness decision is pending; the UI renders the archive-or-discard guard from the refusal and re-dispatches with the chosen resolution. | V2-R01 refusal on pending decision; V2-T01 guard rendered from refusal payload; V2-N01 switch via switcher, palette and menu all pass through the guard | C30 |\n| I2 | Every observed scope is per tenant. Switching tenants never shows another tenant's Inbox items, reads, reviews, conversations, drafts or rotation state. | V2-R02 scope isolation across two tenants; V2-T02 switcher round-trip leaves per-tenant transient state intact | C30 |\n| I3 | A review is bound to the tenant and resource it was opened for and applies only when the typed name matches, validated in the core. A tenant change invalidates the review. | V2-R03 confirmReview refuses on mismatch and on changed context; V2-T03 Apply disabled until names match | C31 |\n| I4 | Reads are finite. The UI distinguishes \"first read pending\" from \"no activity\", reports per-product partial coverage, and the completion toast is warn-kind whenever any source is partial. | V2-R04 ReadStatus transitions; V2-T04 toast kind and text per coverage; V2-N02 pending state visible on first run | C30 |\n| I5 | API responses are immutable snapshots. A later configuration change never alters an earlier response in history; a newly sent request reflects the change. | V2-R05 history entries hash-stable; V2-T05 topic applied → old response unchanged, new response includes it | C33 |\n| I6 | An issued secret is disclosed only through the host (`RevealSecret` sheet, host clipboard), is held per result for the active context and never persisted; any review exit, including a tenant switch or context retirement, drops it (`ForgetSecret`) and resets the reveal state. A dropped secret is not recoverable; the UI offers to issue another key. | V2-R06 held key dropped on review exit, switch and retirement; Reveal after drop refuses; V2-T06 resumed rotation shows no secret controls | C31 |\n| I7 | Key rotation is the admitted sequence additional key (`Version`) → local deployment acknowledgement → old key `Delete` with readback. Until the old key is removed, the reminder is a distinct Inbox item with its own identity and stated return time and the key table shows both keys. Two timestamps are stored once and labelled distinctly: deployment acknowledged (local statement) and old key removed (remote readback). A failed or unknown issuance never reissues automatically; a rejected or unknown Delete keeps the reminder and leaves the completion timestamp unset; unknown outcomes are shown as unconfirmed with reconciliation, never as complete. | V2-R07 state machine incl. failed/unknown issuance and rejected/unknown delete; V2-T07 Inbox item lifecycle; V2-T08 timestamps labelled and stable after refresh | C31 |\n| I8 | One topmost modal. Siblings of the topmost dialog are `aria-hidden`; Escape closes only the topmost; focus returns to the opener. | V2-T09 stacking; V2-N03 keyboard-only dialog traversal | C28 |\n| I9 | A verdict or severity is colour plus icon plus word, never colour alone. | V2-T10 every verdict renderer emits all three; V2-N04 accessibility tree carries the word | C28 |\n| I10 | Toasts have a kind (ok, info, warn), expire on a timer, and are presentation only: everything a toast says is derivable from an observed scope. With Reduce Motion on, toasts persist until dismissed. | V2-T11 kinds and expiry; V2-N05 Reduce Motion persistence | C28 |\n| I11 | Inbox acknowledge and snooze are per item, core-owned, with the return time stated wherever the action is offered (Home, Inbox row, Inbox detail) and identical across them. | V2-R08 snooze return computed in core; V2-T12 Home and Inbox labels equal | C30 |\n| I12 | A fresh tenant never shows seeded or sample data. Its first Harness send creates a user conversation; its Inbox is empty until the first read lands. | V2-R09 fresh tenant scopes empty; V2-T13 first send creates conversation | C30, C32 |\n\nRules I1, I3, I6 and I7 are authority rules under D014: the core owns the\ndecision, the UI renders it. No invariant may be satisfied by invented data: a\nproduct, tab or operation without an admitted service renders as unavailable\n(DESIGN-V2 §6), and a test that passes only on fixtures for such a capability\nis a specification defect.\n# State ownership\n\nEvery state key the frozen prototype actually holds is listed here, by file\nand by the name the prototype uses, with its owner, where it lives, its\nlifetime, and whether the implementation keeps, renames, derives or drops it.\n\n- **Core** — Rust-owned in desktop-core, exposed through `observe`\n  (persisted in the store or session-held), never in browser storage.\n- **UI** — transient presentation state in a per-screen-family zustand store\n  (shell, wizard, inbox, admin, harness, workbench) or React form state.\n  Lost on reload by design.\n- **Host** — never leaves the native host (secret sheet, clipboard).\n- **Dropped** — not implemented; the reason is in [provenance.md](provenance.md).\n\nThe inventory was produced from `grep -o` over `s.*`, `ts.*` and `f.*` in\n`prototype/R2App.dc.html` and `prototype/R2Onboard.dc.html` (version 134)\nand reconciled by hand; a key missing here is a specification defect.\n\n## R2Onboard — wizard and add-tenant sheet (`s.*`, `s.f.*`)\n\n| Prototype key | Meaning | Owner | Where / intent | Lifetime | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `s.idx`, `s.path`, `s.vi` | current step, step path, visited steps | UI | wizard store | until the wizard closes | kept; `onboarding=inProgress` is set in core when the welcome step is left |\n| `s.f.name` | tenant display name | UI → Core | `completeOnboarding`/`addTenant` | written at completion | kept |\n| `s.env`, `s.region` | environment, region selects | UI → Core | same | same | kept |\n| `s.f.tsg` | TSG id (10 digits) | UI → Core | same | same | kept |\n| `s.f.clientId`, `s.f.secret` | management OAuth client id and secret (required in the prototype) | id: UI → Core; secret: **Host** | id in `addTenant`; secret entered only in the host secret sheet, vault record id returned | secret never in the web layer | **changed** (deviation P17): management credentials become an optional second section under D013; the id is kept, the secret field is replaced by \"Add management client…\" |\n| `s.scanOn`, `s.f.scanKey` | scan key toggle and value | toggle: UI; key: **Host** | \"Add scan key…\" opens the host secret sheet; required for first run (D013) | never in the web layer | **changed** (P1, P17): toggle removed on first run (the scan key is required); kept in add mode |\n| `s.gwOn`, `s.f.ws`, `s.f.endpoint`, `s.f.gwKey` | Gateway section toggle, workspace name, endpoint URL, workspace key | toggle/ws/endpoint: UI → Core; key: **Host** | `addTenant.gateway{ws, endpoint}`; key via secret sheet | — | kept; key path changed (P1) |\n| `s.tried`, `s.vsig`, `s.vsStale` | validation attempted, validation signature, stale flag | UI (tried) / Core (signature, stale) | wizard component; `setup.verify.stale`, `setup.completable` | per step / per draft | kept; the signature and the completion gate moved to the core so the presentation cannot complete over stale results |\n| `s.vs` | verify results per product | Core | `ShellSnapshot.setup.verify` (streamed `VerifyStep`s from `verifyTenantSetup`, with `done`/`failed`/`stale`) | until the draft completes or is discarded | kept; computed in core, including staleness against a signature of the draft's inputs |\n| `s.aesFixed`, `s.failCleared` | AES warning demo acknowledged; failed row cleared | UI | wizard store | per run | **changed**: AES is not an admitted product (P18); the warning demo becomes a generic \"warning row\" fixture on an admitted product |\n| `s.prods` | selected products | UI → Core | `completeOnboarding.products` | written at completion | kept; unavailable products are not selectable (P18) |\n| `s.packs`, `s.casesState` | starter packs selected, pack state | UI → Core | `completeTenantSetup.products` → the bundled catalog's cases as library entries named by their starter pack, Not run (P55) | written at completion | kept |\n| `s.acad`, `s.af` (`f.email`, `f.dname`), `s.streaks`, `s.remind` | local Academy account form, streaks, reminders | **Dropped** / Core (Academy) | Academy identity is hosted (D004–D006, C04); the step signs in or skips | — | **changed** (P19): no local account, streaks or reminders in the wizard |\n| `s.help` | help popover open | UI | wizard store | transient | kept |\n| `s.opened` | \"Open Prisma AIRS\" progress | UI | mirrors the `onboarding` transition | transient | kept |\n| `s.theme` | theme prop | Core | `appearance` | — | kept (observed) |\n\n## R2App — shell (`s.*`)\n\n| Prototype key | Meaning | Owner | Where | Lifetime | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `s.tenant`, `s.byTenant` | active tenant; per-tenant state bag | Core | `ShellSnapshot.active`, per-tenant scopes below | persisted | kept; the bag is replaced by scopes |\n| `s.screen` | destination | Core | `navigate` intent, `ShellSnapshot.features` | session | kept; feature ids gain `home`, `inbox` |\n| `s.theme` | theme | Core | `appearance` | persisted | kept |\n| `s.wizard`, `s.added` | first-run wizard open; add-tenant sheet result | Core / UI | `ShellSnapshot.onboarding`; sheet result is a toast | — | kept |\n| `s.overlayDone` | prototype overlay bookkeeping | **Dropped** | canvas-only | — | dropped |\n| `s.palette`, `s.pq` | palette open, query | UI | shell store | transient | kept |\n| `s.tenantOpen`, `s.guard` | switcher open; switch guard | UI | shell store; guard content from the `SwitchBlocked` refusal (`to`, `pending`, `completion`), rendered by the shell for a switch and by the wizard for a setup completion | transient | kept (I1); completion is the same guarded path (P36) |\n| `s.toast`, `s.toastKind`, `s.undo` | toast text, kind, undo target | UI | shell store, timer | ≤ 6 s | kept (I10) |\n| `s.detail`, `s.sheet` | detail sheet; confirm sheet | UI | shell store modal stack | transient | kept (I8) |\n| `s.explain` | explain-mode toggle | UI | shell store | transient | kept |\n| `s.sel`, `s.itab` | Inbox selection, tab | UI | shell store, keyed by tenant id | transient; a switch round trip keeps it (I2) | kept |\n| `s.product`, `s.exp`, `s.ptab`, `s.allOpen`, `s.adminNav`, `s.adminQ`, `s.tree` | Administration product, expanded set, tab per product, expand-all, nav mode, search, tree | UI | admin store | transient | kept |\n| `s.review`, `s.reviewKind`, `s.reviewFrom`, `s.reviewTenant`, `s.reviewApplied` | review open, kind, origin, bound tenant, applied | Core | `Review` scope (tenant-bound, invalidated on context change) | session | kept; the UI holds only \"open\" |\n| `s.typed` | typed confirmation | UI | admin store | until review exit | kept (I3) |\n| `s.secretVisible` | reveal toggle | **Host** | `RevealSecret` sheet; UI holds nothing | 60 s sheet | **changed** (P2): no web reveal state |\n| `s.credOpen`, `s.credVal` | credential dialog open, value | Host | credential entry is the host secret sheet | — | **changed** (P1): no web credential value |\n| `s.packsOpen`, `s.packPick` | starter-pack dialog, picks | UI → Core | library intent | transient | kept |\n| `s.ncOpen`, `s.ncName`, `s.ncPid`, `s.ncInput`, `s.ncDir` | new-case dialog fields | UI → Core | `createCase` (existing library) | transient | kept |\n| `s.casePage`, `s.caseQ`, `s.cfil` | Test Cases paging, search, filter | UI | Test Cases screen state (keyed by context) | transient | kept |\n| `s.req`, `s.reqQ`, `s.fmt`, `s.rtab`, `s.rsub` | API Client request, search, body format, response tab, sub-tab | UI | workbench store | transient | kept |\n| `s.quiz` | Academy quiz state | **Dropped** | hosted Academy owns it (P19) | — | dropped |\n| `s.convoHint` | ended-conversation hint | UI | harness store; cleared by any navigation; its opening question is the core's `opening` (the conversation's, or the opened saved record's), never the title (P51) | transient | kept |\n| (none; DESIGN-V2 §3) | \"Show the calls\" preference and panel | Core | `ShellSnapshot.showCalls` (the operator's choice once made, else on until onboarding completes) and `ShellSnapshot.calls` (the active tenant's calls this app run: request line, credential kind, header names, status, duration; never a value, query or body) (P54) | preference persisted; calls in memory for the app run | added |\n| `ts.activeCase` hand-off | the case opened in the Playground | UI → Core | shell store `playground` (tenant, case, fields) → Runtime Playground prefill; the scan names the case (`scan.case`) and the core records its result (P55, P56) | until the next hand-off | kept |\n\n## R2App — per-tenant (`ts.*`)\n\n| Prototype key | Meaning | Owner | Where | Lifetime | Disposition |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `ts.reading`, `ts.reads`, `ts.firstRead`, `ts.lastRead`, `ts.warnCleared`, `ts.capsScan` | read in progress, count, first read done, last-read time, warning cleared, scan capability | Core | `ReadStatus` scope, from the tenant's read record (one per opened tenant, kept across a switch round trip, P37) | session, per tenant | kept (I4); `reads` count is not shown and never feeds a timestamp |\n| `ts.ack`, `ts.snoozed` | acknowledgments, snoozes with return | Core | `InboxEntry.hold` (`acknowledge`, or `snooze` with `returnsAt` computed in the core); intents `acknowledgeItem`/`snoozeItem`/`unsnoozeItem`; the last snooze's `SnoozeReceipt` on the Inbox scope is what the toast announces, once (P38) | session, per tenant (as v1's holds) | kept (I11); not persisted across a relaunch, as the retained holds were not |\n| `ts.prompt`, `ts.dir`, `ts.profile`, `ts.model`, `ts.app`, `ts.kind`, `ts.scanned`, `ts.scanDir`, `ts.scanProfile`, `ts.results`, `ts.activeCase`, `ts.activeInput`, `ts.activeDir` | Playground prompt, direction, profile, model, app, classified kind, scanned flag, scan selectors, per-case results, active case | prompt/dir/profile/model/app: UI; scan result + per-case results: Core | workbench store; `ScanRecordView` (existing) and library case results | prompt transient; results persisted per tenant | kept; verdict computed by the existing scan path, never by a UI classifier (the prototype's `classify` is fixture-only) |\n| `ts.extraCases`, `ts.packs` | user-created cases, packs | Core | library | persisted | kept |\n| `ts.hist` | API responses | Core | workbench history | persisted | kept (I5) |\n| `ts.topicRev`, `ts.topicDesc`, `ts.injApplied`, `ts.aesApplied`, `ts.aesRev` | applied review results | Core | read back from the product scopes after apply | — | kept for Runtime topics; `aes*` **dropped** (P18) |\n| `ts.keyRotated`, `ts.keySecretClosed`, `ts.keyDeployed`, `ts.keyDoneAt` | rotation stage | Core | `Tenant.rotations` → `ShellSnapshot.rotations: RotationView{stage, oldKey, newKey, issuedBy, deploymentAcknowledgedAt, oldKeyRemovedAt, removalRejected}`; stage derived (issuanceUnconfirmed, issued, resumed, acknowledged, removalUnconfirmed, complete); intent `acknowledgeDeployment` | per tenant, persisted (timestamps stored once, and the reviewed key name that alone confirms an unconfirmed issuance); the held secret only on its change result, for its context | **changed** (P20, P41): supported Version → Delete sequence; see screens/reviews-administration.md |\n| `ts.follows` | follow-up status per conversation | Core | sessions | persisted | kept |\n| `ts.convo`, `ts.userConvos`, `ts.msgsBy`, `ts.decs`, `ts.ended`, `ts.archived` | current conversation, user conversations, messages, decisions, ended flags, archives | Core | existing harness sessions and decisions | persisted, protected | kept |\n| `ts.draft` | composer draft | UI | shell store per tenant and workspace ID (`harnessDrafts`), with kept messages (`harnessUnsent`: not sent, or not confirmed until their own conversation reports the core took their send ID, `ConversationView.submissions`) | transient | kept |\n\n## Credential entry paths (native)\n\n| Credential | Entry | Storage | Readable by the web layer |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Scan API key | webview password field read through a ref, cleared at once, sent once as `EnteredSecret` inside `configureScanCredential` (P25) | vault (`cr-` record), Keychain v2 namespace | state and mode only (`ScanView`), never the value |\n| Management client secret | same path, `configureManagementCredential` (optional section) | vault | client id and state only |\n| Gateway workspace key | same path, `configureInferenceCredential` | vault | state only |\n| Issued API key (rotation) | `RevealSecret` host sheet / host clipboard | held per result for the active context, never persisted; retired by the review's exit (`DiscardChange` on the applied review, also before the change settles), by acknowledging the deployment, or by a context change | never |\n# Inbox and reads\n\nSource: `R2App` (`ITEMS`, `visibleItems`, `ack`, `snoozed`, `snooze`,\n`inboxRows`, `sel`, `startRead`, `recheck`, `toGo`). Chunk: C30.\n\n## Inbox\n\n- Tabs All / Needs action / Updates / Snoozed with counts. Rows: severity\n  (colour + icon + word), title, product, source line (\"API keys · cached\",\n  \"rotated 07:46\"), when, acknowledged mark. Selecting opens the detail pane:\n  body, facts table, \"Why this matters\" explanation, action button (navigates\n  with context), Acknowledge / Mark as needing action, Snooze or Unsnooze.\n- Empty states distinguish \"first read pending\" from \"nothing needs action\"\n  and name partial sources (\"3 of 6 sources were read at 07:45\").\n- Snooze: per item, core-computed return time, 24 hours for every item in\n  P09 (the prototype's deadline-bound rotation snooze is not carried: no\n  admitted operation has a grace deadline, P20). The label, toast and\n  Snoozed-tab \"Returns …\" text all state the same time (I11). The\n  `deadline` field stays optional for a future item that has a real one.\n- Contextual navigation: an item's action carries a target (screen, product,\n  tab, conversation). Into an ended conversation it sets a transient hint\n  offering \"Open the saved transcript\" and \"Start a new investigation\".\n\n## Reads (I4)\n\n- `readTenant` runs a finite read of every selected product; `ReadStatus`\n  carries per-product state (pending, ok, partial, needsRole), last-read\n  time and counts. First read on a fresh tenant shows pending everywhere.\n- Completion toast: ok \"Read finished at 07:45 · all sources complete\" or\n  warn \"· 3 sources partial\"; for fresh tenants \"· 1 product still needs a\n  role\" or \"· no activity yet in <tenant>\". `recheckProduct` after a role is\n  granted shows an info toast and re-reads.\n\n## Core contract\n\n- Scope `InboxItem{id, kind, severity, title, product, source, when, body,\n  facts, action_target, explanation, deadline?, snooze_return?, acknowledged}`\n  per tenant; intents `acknowledgeItem`, `snoozeItem`, `unsnoozeItem`,\n  `readTenant`, `recheckProduct`.\n- The rotation reminder (`rotation-<application>`) is a distinct item id from\n  the expiry alert and clears only when the old key's deletion is read back\n  (I7). Items exist only for admitted products (DESIGN-V2 §6).\n\n## Fixtures and tests\n\n- Board R2Inbox. Tests V2-R02, R04, R08, R09; V2-T04, T12, T13; V2-N02.\n# Shell, Home, tenant switcher, palette, toasts\n\nSource: `prototype/R2App.dc.html` (sidebar markup, `nav`, `switchPatch`,\n`requestSwitch`, `palette*`, `toast*`, `top`, Home section). Chunk: C28 (shell,\nHome skeleton), C30 (Home attention list backed by Inbox scope).\n\n## Sidebar\n\n- Top: tenant switcher button showing tenant name and environment pill;\n  ⌘T opens the list. Entries: every tenant (name, environment, TSG), then\n  \"Add a tenant…\". Selected tenant uses `sel/selInk`.\n- Destinations in the order of DESIGN-V2 §5 with icon, label, shortcut hint.\n  Unavailable destinations render disabled with their availability reason as\n  a tooltip and accessible description, never hidden.\n- Bottom: appearance toggle (light/dark/system) and \"Show the calls\" toggle,\n  both dispatching their Rust preference.\n- Collapses to icons below 1280px window width.\n\n## Home\n\n- Title \"Home\" with the tenant name and environment beneath; last-read line\n  from `ReadStatus` (\"Read finished at 07:45 · all sources complete\" or\n  \"· 3 sources partial\", warn-styled when partial; \"First read pending\" on a\n  fresh tenant).\n- \"Needs attention\": up to five Inbox items of kind Needs action, each with\n  severity (colour + icon + word), title, product, action button that\n  navigates with context, Acknowledge, and Snooze whose tooltip and\n  accessible name are the item's `snoozeLabel`.\n- Product cards for admitted, licensed products with their read state;\n  unlicensed products are listed once as \"Not licensed in this tenant\" and\n  unavailable products once as \"Not available in this release\" (DESIGN-V2\n  §6), never hidden.\n- Playground card (Runtime Security scan): prompt, direction, profile select,\n  verdict panel (word + icon + reason + plain explanation). Verdicts are\n  profile-aware: a detector not enabled, or a topic not assigned, reads \"Not\n  detected\" with the reason; monitor-only turns Blocked into Flagged with the\n  profile named.\n\n## Tenant switch (I1, I2)\n\n- `switchTenant{tenant_id, resolution?}`; the core refuses with\n  `SwitchBlocked{pending: {conversation, decision}}` when a Harness decision is\n  pending. The UI renders the guard dialog (Archive and switch / Discard and\n  switch / Stay) from that payload and re-dispatches with `resolution`. Initial\n  focus is on Stay, the safe default (Enter on arrival never ends the\n  conversation); Archive and switch stays the visually primary action.\n- Every entry point (switcher, palette, menu, wizard completion) uses the same\n  intent. After a switch the new tenant's Home renders from cached scopes within\n  100 ms with read status pending until the read lands.\n\n## Command palette\n\n- ⌘K. Entries: destinations from the Rust inventory, tenants, and three actions\n  (Navigate, Switch tenant, Read now). Typing filters; Enter dispatches; Escape\n  closes and restores focus. Native menu navigation while the palette is open\n  closes it first.\n\n## Toasts (I10)\n\n- Kinds ok, info, warn with icon and colour; one visible at a time, newest\n  replaces; 6 s expiry (persist under Reduce Motion); optional Undo action\n  (snooze). A toast never carries information absent from an observed scope.\n\n## Modals (I8)\n\n- Stack order kept in the shell store; only the topmost is interactive;\n  siblings get `aria-hidden`; Escape closes the topmost; focus returns to the\n  opener. Detail sheets (transcripts, facts) are modals in this stack.\n\n## Fixtures and tests\n\n- Fixture boards: R2Palette, R2Tenants, R2Dark, R2App default.\n- V2-T01, T02, T09, T10, T11; V2-N01, N03, N04, N05.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
